1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 Nbd7 ({This is not
so popular as} 6... e6 {, but it is solid enough. The deluge of analysis which
followed the repeated adoption of 6...e6 in the Candidates' Tournament seems
to have established the following as the best continuation for both sides:} 7.
f4 Be7 8. Qf3 Nbd7 9. O-O-O Qc7 10. g4 b5 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. g5 Nd7 13. a3 Bb7 {
(so far Gligoric-Fischer, Bled 1959). This game continued} 14. Bh3 ({Gligoric
himself attempted a further refinement of White's play in his game with Bertok
in the 1960 Yugoslav Championship, continuing} 14. h4 O-O-O 15. Bh3 Kb8 16. f5
e5 17. f6 gxf6 18. Nf5 {, with the initiative; but Black's strategy could also
be improved here by utilising Matanovic's [b4] idea.}) 14... O-O-O ({In the
zonal tournament at Budapest, Matanovic against Kostro improved Black's play
by the temporary pawn sacrifice} 14... b4 $1 15. axb4 Qc4 16. Rhg1 Qxb4 17. f5
Ne5 {with satisfactory counter-chances for Black on the queenside.}) 15. f5 $1
Bxg5+ 16. Kb1 e5 17. Ndxb5 $1 {and White's attack was soon decisive.}) 7. Bc4 (
7. f4 {is inferior here because of} Qb6 8. Qd2 ({or} 8. Nb3 Qe3+) 8... Qxb2 9.
Rb1 Qa3 {; and in contrast to the similar variation after 6...e6, White does
not have the attack 10 e5 available.}) 7... e6 ({Black has to be on guard
during the next few moves against the possibility of the sacrifice Bxe6.
Consequently, most masters prefer first} 7... Qa5 8. Qd2 {, and only now} e6 {
. The last word now is still the game Tal-Petrosian, Belgrade 1960, which
continued:} 9. O-O h6 10. Bh4 g5 11. Bg3 Nh5 12. Bxe6 fxe6 13. Nxe6 Nxg3 14.
fxg3 Ne5 15. Rxf8+ (15. Nxf8 Qc5+) 15... Rxf8 16. Qxd6 {, with wild
complications which eventually ended in perpetual check.}) 8. O-O h6 9. Bh4 Ne5
({Uncovering additional protection for the e-pawn and preparing to occupy c4.
If} 9... Qc7 {the sacrifice at e6 is very strong.}) 10. Bb3 Qc7 {An
enterprising and risky plan which is characteristic of Kottnauer's liking for
an active position even at the cost of some pawn weaknesses. Black is prepared
to barter his pawn chain for the two bishops after Bxf6.} 11. Qe2 {White
prefers to entice a different kind of weakness, expecting that Black will
secure his knight on e5 by 11...g5.} Be7 ({It is very risky to lose further
time with his knight in view of his retarded queen's wing development.
Preferable is} 11... g5 12. Bg3 Be7 {, although White maintains the freer
position by} 13. f4) 12. f4 Ng6 13. Bg3 Qc5 {White had quite failed to realise
that this move virtually forces a piece sacrifice, since if 14 Bf2 Nxf4 or 14
Qe3 Ng4. In retrospect, however, it is not surprising that a sacrificial line
should be available in view of Black's loss of time with his queen and queen's
knight.} 14. Rad1 e5 15. fxe5 dxe5 16. Bf2 $2 ({The right idea, but the wrong
method. Correct is} 16. Na4 Qa7 17. Bf2 {. This disposes of the resource 17...
Nf4 (18 Qc4) and also makes a similar continuation to the game still better
for White, e.g.} exd4 18. Bxd4 Bg4 ({Finally, if} 18... Qb8 {there is the neat
variation} 19. e5 Bg4 20. Bxf7+ Kf8 21. exf6 Bxe2 22. fxg7#) 19. Qxg4 Nxg4 20.
Bxf7+ Kf8 21. Bxg6+ Kg8 22. Bxa7 {, winning easily.}) 16... exd4 ({An
alternative and quite playable defence is} 16... Nf4 17. Qd2 Qc7 {, when it is
quite hard for White to capitalise on his lead in development, whereas the
knight on f4 is quite well posted.}) 17. Bxd4 Bg4 $2 ({The decisive mistake:
Black intends simplification, not realising that his king faces dangers even
in the ending. Much better is} 17... Qg5 18. e5 Bg4 19. Qe1 Nxe5 20. Bxe5 Bxd1
21. Bxf6 ({Stockfish 15:} 21. Bd6 $11) 21... Qc5+ ({Stockfish 15:} 21... gxf6
$17) 22. Kh1 gxf6 23. Qxd1 Rd8 {, when Black remains the exchange up, although
his exposed king may still cause him trouble.}) ({Not} 17... Qh5 18. Bxf7+) 18.
Bxf7+ ({Still simpler is the immediate} 18. Qxg4) 18... Kf8 ({If} 18... Kxf7
19. Qxg4 Qa5 ({Stockfish 15:} 19... Qg5 20. Qxg5 hxg5 21. e5 $16) 20. e5 ({
Stockfish 15:} 20. Nd5 $18) 20... Nxe5 21. Qh5+ {, regaining the piece with
the better game; nevertheless this was the best chance.}) 19. Qxg4 Nxg4 20.
Bxg6+ Kg8 21. Bxc5 Bxc5+ 22. Kh1 Ne3 ({No better is} 22... Nf2+ 23. Rxf2 Bxf2
24. g4) 23. g4 $1 {The winning move, for Black is prevented from developing
his h8-rook via h6-h5 and Rh6. The remainder of the game is easy for White;
for the black forces, totally paralysed, are reduced to helpless shuffling
while the white king strolls over and picks up the queenside pawns.} Nxf1 24.
Rxf1 Rf8 ({Losing more quickly than necessary, but if} 24... Rd8 25. Nd5 {and}
Bd4 {is unplayable because of} 26. Ne7#) 25. Rxf8+ Kxf8 26. Nd5 Bd6 27. Kg2 Be5
28. b3 Bd6 29. h4 h5 30. gxh5 Rh6 31. Kf3 Rh8 32. Kg4 Rh6 33. Kf5 Bg3 34. Ke6
Bxh4 35. c4 Bg3 36. Kd7 Be5 37. Kc8 {Source: BCM, July 1960, p213} 1-0

