{Position after 20.Rf1...} 20... Qd4 $1 21. Nxe6 (21. Bxe7 Ba4 22. Kc1 Nb3+ $13
) 21... Qxd3+ $1 22. cxd3 Ba4+ 23. Kc1 Nb3+ 24. Kc2 $2 (24. Kb1 Nd2+ 25. Kc1 {
"draws, but naturally enough White wanted to win" (PH Clarke in BCM, who also
tells us that "Barden was already very short of time")}) 24... Re2+ 25. Kxc3
d4+ 26. Kb4 b5 $2 ({PH Clarke: "looking at the game again a couple of days
later to see whether there was anything else Black could have done, I
discovered} 26... Nc6+ 27. Kxa4 Rb2 $1 {threatening 28...Nc5+ 29.Nxc5 b5 mate."
} 28. Rac1 Nxc1 29. Nc7 Nb3 {is Clarke's analysis and he goes on to work out a
winning line for Black. "When I (PHC) asked Barden whether he had thought of
this, he said he had but in his haste had noticed that White's knight
prevented ...Nc5 mate and had seen no further. What a pity, for I believe that
White has no satisfactory defence against all the threats to his king... if
this is correct and had Barden so gained a great victory, it must surely have
been regarded as an immortal game." However, engines give} 30. Rc1 $1 Nxc1 31.
Bxd8 Nb3 32. Qf5+ Kxd8 33. Ne6+ Ke8 34. Ng7+ Kf8 35. Qc8+ Kxg7 36. Qg4+ {
with perpetual.}) ({Despite overlooking the defensive strength of 30.Rc1,
Clarke's instincts were right. Analysis engines find the right way to
immortality for Black using a different move order:} 26... Rb2 $3 {when Black
does indeed win:} 27. Nxd8 Nd5+ 28. Kc4 Nc3 29. Qf5+ Kc7 30. Ne6+ fxe6 31. Qh7+
Bd7 {, etc.}) 27. Rac1+ Nxc1 28. Rxc1+ {Source: BCM, July 1960, p190} 1-0

