1. e4 c5 2. c3 {A favourite move of Milner-Barry's, with which he has obtained
several convincing wins. Black's choice of reply lies between 2...Nf6, 2...d5,
2...g6 and the move selected.} e6 3. d4 d5 4. e5 Qb6 5. Bd3 Bd7 ({If} 5... cxd4
6. cxd4 Bd7 7. Nf3 Nc6 {, Milner-Barry would have played a gambit continuation
of his own making:} 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 {. If Black accepts the
second pawn by} Qxe5 ({while if} 10... Qb6 11. Qg4 {and it is difficult for
Black to complete his development}) 11. Re1 Qd6 (11... Qb8 12. Nxd5) 12. Nb5
Bxb5 13. Bxb5+ Kd8 14. Qh5 {, White has a powerful and possibly decisive attack
}) 6. dxc5 ({Here} 6. Nf3 Bb5 7. O-O Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Qa6 {has proved to be quite
good for Black, since the exchange of the light-squared bishops takes the
sting from White's attack on the kingside.}) 6... Bxc5 7. Qe2 a5 {Otherwise
White gains a lot of territory by 8.b4 followed by Be3.} 8. Nf3 Ne7 9. Nbd2 Ng6
({This apparently logical attack on the e-pawn fails to White's energetic
reply. Better is} 9... a4 10. b4 axb3 11. Nxb3 Ba4 {(Milner-Barry-Clarke,
Ilford 1953), with about equal chances. White has some advantage in space but
his a-pawn and c-pawn are weak.}) 10. Nb3 Be7 11. h4 f6 {Hoping for
counterplay with the central pawn mass.} ({The late grandmaster Nimzovitch, a
specialist in the opening, showed on many occasions that the advance of the
h-pawn to h6 can effectively disorganise Black's position. For instance, if} 
11... Nc6 12. h5 Nf8 13. h6 g6 14. Bg5 {, and White has excellent chances of
exploiting the weaknesses on the black squares.}) 12. h5 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 fxe5 14.
h6 {Very powerful, for the pawn acts as a permanent thorn in Black's flesh.} g6
({If now} 14... e4 15. hxg7) 15. Qxe5 Rg8 (15... Rf8 {would be met by the
elegant queening combination} 16. Be3 Qd6 17. Qxd6 ({Engines dislike this and
give} 17. Qg7 $1 $18 {, which was the move which Milner-Barry gave in his note
in BCM - JS}) 17... Bxd6 18. Bxg6+ hxg6 19. h7 Be5 20. Bd4 {- an illustration
of how strong White's h-pawn has already become. [but engines continue} Bxd4
21. Nxd4 Rh8 {and consider that Black is winning - JS}) 16. Be3 Qd6 17. Bxg6+ (
{A quieter line which is also very strong is} 17. Qxd6 Bxd6 18. f4 {, but
Milner-Barry revels in complications.}) 17... Rxg6 18. Qh8+ Bf8 19. Qxh7 e5 ({
Black has no time for} 19... Rxg2 20. Qd3 Rg8 21. h7 Rh8 22. Qg6+ Kd8 23. Bg5+)
20. O-O-O ({Continuing the attack with the threat of Rxd5 and bringing his
last piece into play. If instead} 20. Qh8 Nc6 21. h7 O-O-O 22. Qg8 Be7 {
and Black escapes.}) 20... Bc6 21. Bc5 Rxh6 ({The only hope, for if} 21... Qf6
22. Bxf8 Kxf8 {, White wins neatly by} 23. Qxg6 Qxg6 24. h7) 22. Qg8 ({Not} 22.
Bxd6 Rxh7 23. Rxh7 Bxd6 {, when Black's two bishops come into the game and it
is he who has the winning chances.}) 22... Rg6 23. Qh8 Qf6 24. Bxf8 Qxf8 $2 ({
After defending well, Black falters and exposes his king to the full crossfire
of White's pieces. He should try} 24... Qxh8 25. Rxh8 Kf7 {when the ending is
very difficult to win because of the bishops of opposite colours and Black's
useful centre.}) 25. Qxe5+ Kd8 26. Rh8 Rg8 27. Nc5 Bd7 {Despair, but the
threat of Ne6+ is deadly. Black could resign here, but plays on to the time
control at move 36.} 28. Nxd7 Nxd7 29. Qxd5 Kc7 30. Qxd7+ Kb6 31. Qd4+ Ka6 32.
Qd6+ Qxd6 33. Rxd6+ b6 34. Rxg8 Rxg8 35. g3 Rh8 36. Kc2 {Sources: Manchester
Guardian, 16 June 1960, p6; BCM, July 1960, p215; CHESS, Vol.25, no.365-6,
July 1960, p276} 1-0

